  fitting the SAME modified Schechter form to the framework's predicted width function over 25-500 km/s gives log phi* = -1.79, w* = 239 km/s, alpha = -1.26, beta = 2.40
  the published alpha.100 values are log phi* = -1.67, w* = 307 km/s, alpha = -0.63 (+1.12/-0.41), beta = 2.0
  the framework's ANALYTIC low-width slope is 4(alpha_HI + 1)/(d log M_b/d log M_HI) = 4 x -0.29/1.13 = -1.03, which is -1.0 sigma from the published value on the all-statistical error bar
  the like-for-like number is the FITTED alpha = -1.26 (same functional form, same width range as the paper); the raw Monte-Carlo 30-100 km/s log-slope is -1.57, steeper still because the turbulence floor piles sources up near 20-30 km/s
  [PASS] 32.2c AGAINST INTEREST, the shape: the framework predicts a STEEPER low-width slope than ALFALFA measures, on every way of reading it.  Fitting the paper's own functional form to the framework's predicted width function gives alpha = -1.26 against the published -0.63: a 1.5 sigma tension on the published all-statistical error bar and a 4 sigma one on its counting-only error bar.  This check asserts the TENSION and would fail if the slopes agreed   (fitted -1.26 vs published -0.63 (+1.12/-0.41) = -1.5 sigma all-stat, and vs -0.56 +- 0.16 counting-only = -4.3 sigma; analytic value -1.03, raw 30-100 km/s slope -1.57)
  the likeliest explanation is NOT a_0: it is that a 50% line width is not a flat rotation speed, and the mapping between them is mass dependent.  Fits of the BTFR with W50 give a slope near 3.3 where fits with V_flat give 4 (Bradford+16; Lelli+16; Ponomareva+18).  A slope of 3.3 instead of 4 is exactly a shallower velocity function, and the framework has no way to distinguish itself from that systematic using widths alone.

