5. MUTATION CONTROLS
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  [PASS] M32a mutation: a_0 raised by 4x must slide the predicted ROTATIONAL widths by exactly 0.25 log10(4) = +0.151 dex, and it does -- so the amplitude comparison in section 2 really is an a_0 measurement and not a shape coincidence.  (On the raw widths the shift is diluted to about +0.10 dex by the 20 km/s turbulence floor, which is itself a reminder that the narrow end of this test is floor-dominated)   (rotational widths move +0.1502 dex against the exact +0.1505; raw widths above 100 km/s move +0.0341 dex; raw widths overall move +0.1136)
    and it costs accuracy: at w = 50 km/s the 4a_0 mutation gives a ratio of 2.44 against the framework's 1.69
  [PASS] M32b mutation: replacing the measured HI mass function with a FLAT one changes the predicted low-width slope by more than a full unit -- so section 2 is testing the mass function's SHAPE carried through the framework's map, not an insensitive integral that any input would satisfy   (flat input gives a 30-100 km/s log-slope of -0.36 against the framework's -1.57 and the observed -0.63)

======================================================================================================================
VERDICT -- ITEM 32: a partial pass, and the strongest thing in it is the comparison, not the fit
======================================================================================================================
  The framework converts the published ALFALFA HI mass function into the published ALFALFA HI width function with
  ZERO free parameters -- a_0 from Lambda, the baryon correction and the inclination distribution both measured -- and
  lands within a factor 0.95-1.69 over w50 = 50-150 km/s, against an irreducible 0.12 dex floor set by the two
  published fits' own mutual normalisation.  It is INSIDE that floor at 80 and 120 km/s and outside it at 50 km/s,
  where it over-predicts by +0.23 dex -- a real miss, not a rounding.
  AND THE AMPLITUDE IS NOT INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE: it is the BTFR zero-point (+0.010 dex in log v on the same
  catalogue) re-expressed as number densities.  The independent content of this item is the SHAPE and the LambdaCDM
  comparison.  The zero-point is itself measured on width-selected detections, which biases it low (see h31b).
  At 30 km/s it over-predicts by 3.0 x with the Schechter extrapolated to 1e6
  and UNDER-predicts by 0.33 x truncated at ALFALFA's own 1e7 floor, so the item's 'within errors at 30-100 km/s'
  is not established at the bottom end -- and that point is set by where the mass function stops, not by a_0.
  The predicted low-width slope, -1.26 fitted the paper's own way (-1.03 analytically), is steeper than the
  measured -0.63; that is a real tension of 1.5 to 4 sigma depending on which published error bar is used, and the honest
  reading is the velocity-definition systematic (W50 vs V_flat gives BTFR slopes 3.3 vs 4), not a_0.
  What is NOT ambiguous is the alternative computed beside it.  LambdaCDM's halo velocity function, built here from
  scratch and validated on the cluster abundance, overshoots the same measured width function by 11 x at 50 km/s and
  35 x at 30 -- the known 'velocity function problem', reproduced independently.  The framework is an order of
  magnitude closer at both, and it is closer WITHOUT a halo occupation function, a V_max-to-W50 correction, or any
  free parameter.  Recorded as a WORKS on the comparison and a partial miss on the absolute shape.

  Two corrections made in audit, both stated rather than buried:
   * the LambdaCDM halos were being drawn with weights proportional to dn/dlogV over a uniform log-MASS grid, which
     re-weights by the local slope of V_max(M).  Fixed to dn/dlogM.  The slope is nearly constant so the error was
     about 1% -- it moved the 50 km/s excess from 11.1x to 11.2x -- but it was an error.
   * check 32.2a originally compared the 50-120 km/s ratios against a hand-picked factor of 1.8 while the worst was
     1.69.  That threshold was doing work it had not earned; it is now compared against the 0.125 dex floor that
     check 32.0 measures from the two published fits BEFORE any framework number is computed, and on that footing
     the 50 km/s point FAILS and is reported as a miss.
  Literature inputs verified this session against Oman 2022 MNRAS 509, 3268 Table 1: the alpha.100 HIMF
  (log phi* = -2.26, log M* = 9.92, alpha = -1.29) and HIWF (log phi* = -1.67, w* = 307, alpha = -0.63, beta = 2.0,
  all-statistical; 300/-0.56/2.1 counting-only) and the 21827-source sample size all match the published values.

